39. zradhò, zrâi زرز, the cuirass.
kuiris, grivpân (Pers. giribân), lit. "defense of the neck," hauberk: "that which joins the helmet to the cuirass."
paitidânò, padtân; zak î azêr zrâi yâtûnad, "he who goes under the cuirass," as opposed to the other paitidâna, that of the face: see note 31.
sâravârò, targ, "the helmet." Lit. doubtless "head-circle"; not to be confused with the homonym that gave the Persian shalvâr, breeches. Isidore of Seville distinguishes the two senses of sarabara, breeches and hat (Forcellini, s. v.).
kamara, kamar, belt.
râna-pânò, rân-pân, "thigh-guard."
40. aêsha yuyô-semi ayazhâna pairi-darezâna; these technical expressions and most of those that follow are among the most obscure: they contain many hapax legomena and the Pahlavi text is too corrupt to draw anything certain from it. The following seem certain: aêsha, the plowshare, Pers. khêsh (Pahlavi êsh); pairi-darezâna "attached" is said of an object "attached from the plowshare to the yoke," and must refer to the body of the implement itself: I suspect the yoke is in yuyô-semi and the body in ayazhâna.
41. gavâzishta gavâzò, lit. "the ox-pusher of the herdsman" (?).
42. asmana hâvana; gloss: "any instrument for making small and fine (tak u kam) the vâfrik" (grains? see p. 81, n. 88).
43. yâvarenem uzgeresnô-vaghdhanem; the first term is derived from yava, for it is translated iôrtâî ârd "of wheat, of flour"; usgeresnò, gart, round. Gloss: "hand-mill."
